      "answer": {
        "coverage": "complete",
        "insufficient_evidence": false,
        "summary": "Grenier recommends checking fit across customer needs, company goals, and channel strengths; assessing the channel’s maturity and your company’s readiness; then testing with limited, proportionate effort and predefined signs of progress.",
        "summary_citation_ids": [
          "S1",
          "S2",
          "S3",
          "S5",
          "S7",
          "S8",
          "S9",
          "S10"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "heading": "Assess fit and readiness",
            "content": "First, look for overlap among what customers need, what the company wants to achieve, and what the channel does well. For example, audio-first Clubhouse seemed like a better fit for Spotify’s music and engagement goals than photo-driven Paparazzi; influencer marketing suits highly targeted contextual promotion, while OTT suits broad reach and video storytelling. Next, consider the channel’s “DNA,” including where it is in its trajectory—whether it is established, still developing, or attracting attention without durable potential. Finally, assess your company’s risk appetite and staffing, and whether you have a foundation in more basic channels such as Google and Facebook. Grenier generally advises against making an unproven channel the first priority before that foundation is in place.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S1",
              "S2",
              "S3",
              "S4",
              "S5"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Match investment to the opportunity",
            "content": "Use those factors to size the test rather than committing the whole team by default. Grenier’s examples: a promising but uncertain fit for a small-to-midsize team with only one working channel might merit half of one person’s time; a strong fit, a more mature channel, and a 20-person team could justify three dedicated people. He recommends keeping the initial commitment small, and expanding to a sprint or two for the whole team only if early work shows promise. Consider this alongside other opportunities in your sprint backlog—the effort has an opportunity cost.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S7",
              "S9",
              "S10"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Define evidence and a stopping point",
            "content": "Decide in advance what progress would look like, and use measures appropriate to the channel rather than expecting every test to yield statistically significant results. For Clubhouse, Grenier suggested tracking whether room size increases by 10% each time; a decline from 20 attendees to 15 could signal weak execution, limited channel performance, or insufficient reach. For TikTok, clicks and conversions may be trackable like other channels. New channels often need more than a couple of cycles because there are no established playbooks, but if after more than a quarter the test is not directionally improving or becoming interesting, he would put it on ice for a while. Grenier also estimated that emerging channels work only about 5% of the time, and said brand-new channels rarely deliver the hoped-for early payoff.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S8",
              "S9",
              "S11"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "missing_topics": []
      },
